.TH STYLE 9
.SH NAME
style \- the prose the tree is written in
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tools/ste-lint.py
[
.B \-\-strict
]
[
.B \-\-show
]
[
.B \-\-rule
.I name
]
[
.I path ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Vectra's comments are its design documents.
A doc comment often carries the only record of a decision,
so the tree writes its prose under
.BR ASD-STE100 ,
Simplified Technical English,
a standard built so that a reader cannot misread an instruction.
.I Style
names the rules that apply, the ones a program checks, and the ones it does not.
.SS Strict and STE-flavored
The standard has a strict mode and a relaxed one.
Strict applies to the strings the kernel itself prints:
a
.B fail
reason,
a panic message,
an
.B Error
description,
the name of a check in a
.B verify_
file,
and every line that reaches
.IR klog .
Nobody reads these with the source open,
so every rule applies, including a 20-word cap on the sentence.
.PP
STE-flavored applies to everything else:
doc comments, line comments, and the markdown under
.BR docs/ .
It keeps every structural rule and drops the standard's one-word-one-meaning
lockdown, because a strict rewrite of a design note reads as a different
document, not a clearer one.
The two modes never disagree about a sentence.
They disagree about how many words a paragraph may spend on one point.
.SS The checked rules
.I Tools/ste-lint.py
checks seven of the standard's fifty-three rules,
the ones a program can decide with no dictionary and no judgement call.
.IP
.EX
semicolon   rule 8.1 bans the mark outright
length      20 words for a strict instruction, 25 for description
perfect     present and past perfect, unless a modal needs the compound
passive     a be-verb, a past participle, and a "by" phrase naming an
            actor the sentence could use as its subject
phrasal     a two-word verb whose meaning the parts do not predict
marketing   an adjective that claims a quality instead of showing it
paragraph   one topic, six sentences
.EE
.PP
The checker reads prose, not code:
block and line comments in
.B .odin
files, and the body text of
.B .md
files.
A quoted identifier or a declaration is not a sentence and is skipped.
A markdown table and the tree's aligned two-column display blocks are a
layout, so the length and paragraph rules skip them, though a semicolon in
one still fails rule 8.1.
.PP
.B \-\-strict
also checks the strings the kernel prints.
.B \-\-show
prints every finding.
.B \-\-rule
limits the run to the named rules.
The tree is at zero findings.
A finding is a regression, and the fix is the sentence, not the checker.
.SS What is not checked
The standard's lexical rules are the other half of it:
an approved-word dictionary of about nine hundred words,
each pinned to one meaning and one part of speech.
That dictionary is not free to redistribute, so it is not in this tree and
the checker does not encode it.
What is left is the principle behind it: pick the plainest word that works,
and use it the same way every time, applied by hand and checked by reading.
.PP
One verb form is worth naming because the checker stays quiet about it.
STE permits an
.B \-ing
word only as a technical noun, never as a verb form, so
.B "retrying would be shorter"
wants to be
.BR "a retry would be shorter" .
Reliable detection needs a part-of-speech tagger.
This one is fixed by eye.
.SS The dictionary
The standard lets an organization define its own technical terms,
and this tree uses the allowance for two kinds of entry:
a word pinned to one meaning, and a pair of words a reader would otherwise
take for synonyms.
.IP
.EX
check       one assertion in a self-test, and the verb for making it, not
            "verify", "confirm", "assert" or "ensure"
park        take a thread off every run queue until something wakes it
hold        a thread has a lock right now
slot        one entry in a fixed-size pool, addressed by index
tick        one timer interrupt, the unit of every deadline
slice       the run of ticks a thread gets before the scheduler preempts it
.EE
.PP
.B "hand over"
names a lock moving straight to a waiter without ever being free.
It stays a two-word verb against the phrasal rule, because it names the
mechanism.
.PP
Three pairs look like synonyms and are not.
.B Ready
wakes a thread that waited on something outside itself and gives its
priority back.
.B Unpark
wakes a thread that queued for a lock and does not.
A
.B chan
is this kernel's handle on an open file.
A
.B fid
is the number the 9P server on the other end knows it by.
One chan holds one fid.
A
.B Rendez
transfers nothing on a wake, so the sleeper loops and re-checks its
condition.
A
.B Mutex
transfers the thing being waited for, so a woken thread checks nothing.
A
.I check
is an assertion that runs every boot.
A
.I control
is a deliberate mutation of the code, run once by hand,
to find out whether a check notices.
.SS Departures
Three rules are relaxed on purpose, and named rather than left for a reader
to find and mistake for drift.
The em dash stays, because the standard bans only the semicolon and a dash
here usually marks a sentence the length rule would flag anyway.
Present perfect stays where it carries information the simple past cannot:
.B "the job has completed"
and
.B "the job completed"
are different claims,
and a hedge such as
.B "may have failed"
keeps its auxiliary because the simple form asserts a failure nothing
observed.
Where the tense rule and this modality rule disagree, modality wins.
A display table is not a sentence, so the length and paragraph rules skip
one, though not rule 8.1.
.SS Comments
A file comment says what the file is and why, in prose, at the top of the
file.
It is not a changelog and does not name the commit or the milestone that
added the file.
A line or block comment explains a reason a reader could not get from the
code alone: a constraint, an invariant, or why the obvious approach is
wrong here.
It does not restate what a well-named identifier already says.
.PP
This manual follows the same prose rules, on top of the
macro and section rules in
.IR man (6).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B tools/ste-lint.py
the checker
.SH SOURCE
.B tools/ste-lint.py
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR man (6),
